<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page1-nash-definition" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Nash Equilibrium and Two Important Examples</title>

  <p>
    It's time to learn about one of the biggest concepts in game theory: the
    Nash equilibrium!
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-1">
    <title>Section 3.1, Big Monkey and Little Monkey 3 and the Definition of
    Nash Equilibria</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.1.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This section introduces the definition of a Nash equilibrium. It's
      essentially a strategy profile where no player has an incentive to
      change their strategy, which is where the term <q>equilibrium</q> comes
      from.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    We'll take a moment to rewrite all the notation just introduced, so you
    have it in one place for reference. It might also be helpful to reread it
    here now.
  </p>

  <p>
    We write <m>s^* = (s_1^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m> for the strategy profile we
    are testing to determine whether it's an equilibrium. The profile
    <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium provided:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        For every <m>s_1 \in S_1</m>, <m>\pi_1(s_1^*, s_2^*, \ldots, s_n^*)
        \geq \pi_1(s_1, s_2^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m> (in the compact notation:
        <m>\pi_1(s^*) \geq \pi_1(s_1, s^*_{-1})</m>).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        For every <m>s_2 \in S_2</m>, <m>\pi_2(s_1^*, s_2^*, \ldots, s_n^*)
        \geq \pi_2(s_1^*, s_2, s_3^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m>
        (<m>\pi_2(s^*) \geq \pi_2(s_2, s^*_{-2})</m>).
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        And so on, down to
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        For every <m>s_n \in S_n</m>, <m>\pi_n(s_1^*, \ldots, s_n^*)
        \geq \pi_n(s_1^*, \ldots, s_{n-1}^*, s_n)</m>
        (<m>\pi_n(s^*) \geq \pi_n(s_n, s^*_{-n})</m>).
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Let's put the first bullet point into words: if player 1 alone switches
    to some other strategy <m>s_1</m>, while everyone else stays exactly where
    they are, then player 1's payoff <em>will not go up</em>.
  </p>

  <p>
    The quickest way to write the definition is with the more compact version:
    <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium if for each <m>i = 1, \ldots, n</m> it
    holds that <m>\pi_i(s^*) \geq \pi_i(s_i, s^*_{-i})</m> for every
    <m>s_i \in S_i</m>. However, you should also have a good sense of what
    this means conceptually in words, which is tested in the check below!
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-nash-in-words">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium of some game. Which of the
        following says what that means?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If the players are all at <m>s^*</m>, then every player is getting
            the highest payoff available to them anywhere in the game.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is much stronger than the definition. A player's payoff
            depends on what everyone else does, so the highest payoff
            available to them somewhere in the game may not be reachable by
            anything they can do themselves. We'll see a game shortly where
            both players would rather be somewhere else entirely.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If the players are all at <m>s^*</m>, then no player can raise
            their own payoff by changing strategy on their own, with everyone
            else staying put.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Notice the two things this sentence is careful about: the
            change is made by a single player, and everyone else is held
            fixed.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Each player's strategy in <m>s^*</m> gives them a higher payoff
            than their other strategies, no matter what the other players do.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That's the definition of a dominant strategy from Chapter 2, not
            of a Nash equilibrium. The difference is the phrase <q>no matter
            what the other players do.</q> The Nash condition only compares
            payoffs while the other players are held fixed at
            <m>s^*_{-i}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Section 3.1 also defines a <term>strict</term> Nash equilibrium, which is
    the same idea with <m>\geq</m> replaced by <m>\gt</m>. Note that every
    strict Nash equilibrium is a Nash equilibrium, but not every Nash
    equilibrium is strict.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-strict-nash">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>s^*</m> is a Nash equilibrium. What else must be true for
        <m>s^*</m> to be a <em>strict</em> Nash equilibrium?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            At least one of the inequalities in the definition is strict,
            meaning some player would do strictly worse by changing their
            strategy on their own.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite. Strict means <em>every</em> inequality is strict, not
            just one of them. A profile where one player would do strictly
            worse by deviating, but another player has a tie, is still a Nash
            equilibrium, just not a strict one.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It must be the only Nash equilibrium of the game.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Strictness is about the inequalities at this one profile, not
            about how many equilibria the game has. You'll see shortly that
            Stag Hunt has two Nash equilibria, and both of them are strict.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Each inequality in the definition is strict. That is, every
            player, changing to any other strategy of their own while everyone
            else stays put, gets a strictly lower payoff.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Every inequality, for every player and every alternative
            strategy that player has.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Now let's actually practice using the definition. Section 3.2 finds Nash
    equilibria by <em>inspection</em>, which means checking the definition for
    every strategy profile. The two widgets below will walk you through all
    four profiles of each of the first two examples in that section. The first
    one is the familiar Prisoner's Dilemma! After you answer each question,
    press <q>Next profile</q> at the bottom to move on to the next strategy
    profile.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-pd-inspection">
    <caption>
      Inspecting all four strategy profiles of the Prisoner's Dilemma. At each
      profile, decide whether either executive can do better by changing
      strategy on their own.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-pd-inspection"
      iframe="ch3-graph/prisoners-dilemma-inspection.html" width="100%" aspect="5:6">
      <static>
        <p>
          A guided walkthrough of the Prisoner's Dilemma payoff matrix, with
          Executive 1 choosing the row and Executive 2 the column. Each of the
          four strategy profiles is tested in turn: the profile under
          consideration is highlighted, the two cells reachable by a single
          executive changing strategy are marked, and a multiple choice
          question asks which executives, if any, are better off switching.
          After each answer the relevant payoff comparisons are displayed as
          inequalities and the cell is labeled as a Nash equilibrium or not.
          Three profiles fail the definition and one, (talk, talk), passes,
          making it the unique Nash equilibrium of the game.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    As you just saw, the Prisoner's Dilemma has exactly one Nash equilibrium,
    and it leads to a bad outcome for both executives. Keep that in mind as
    you work through the next game, which behaves quite differently! This next
    example is <q>Stag Hunt,</q> and more context about the setup will come in
    the reading that follows.
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-stag-hunt-inspection">
    <caption>
      The same procedure applied to Stag Hunt. This time more than one profile
      survives.
    </caption>
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-stag-hunt-inspection"
      iframe="ch3-graph/stag-hunt-inspection.html" width="100%" aspect="5:6">
      <static>
        <p>
          A guided walkthrough of the Stag Hunt payoff matrix, with Hunter 1
          choosing the row and Hunter 2 the column. All four strategy profiles
          are tested in the same way as in the Prisoner's Dilemma
          walkthrough. Two profiles, (stag, stag) and (hare, hare), satisfy
          the definition and are strict Nash equilibria. Both hunters prefer
          (stag, stag), but a hunter who switches to the stag alone cannot
          catch it and sees their payoff fall from 1 to 0, which is what makes
          the worse equilibrium difficult to escape.
        </p>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="check-comparing-pd-stag">
    <statement>
      <p>
        You've now found the Nash equilibria of two games by inspection. The
        Prisoner's Dilemma had one, <m>(\text{talk}, \text{talk})</m>, paying
        <m>(-6,-6)</m>, even though both executives would rather have
        <m>(-1,-1)</m>. Stag Hunt had two, and both hunters prefer
        <m>(\text{stag}, \text{stag})</m> to <m>(\text{hare},
        \text{hare})</m>. Taken together, what do these two results tell you
        about Nash equilibria?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A Nash equilibrium may or may not be good for the players. It is a
            profile the game can get stuck at, because no player can improve
            their own payoff by changing strategy alone.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the one statement that covers both games at
            once. <q>Stuck</q> is the right word: the definition says nothing
            about whether an equilibrium is a good outcome, only that no
            single player can walk away from it and do better.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A Nash equilibrium is the outcome that is best for all the
            players.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The Prisoner's Dilemma rules this out. Both executives would
            rather have <m>(-1,-1)</m> than the equilibrium payoff
            <m>(-6,-6)</m>, and yet the equilibrium is where the game gets
            stuck.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            A Nash equilibrium is an outcome that no player would rather trade
            for a different one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Again the Prisoner's Dilemma is the counterexample. Both
            executives would happily trade <m>(-6,-6)</m> for
            <m>(-1,-1)</m>. What they cannot do is get there by changing
            strategy on their own, and that's the only thing the definition
            asks about.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Let's think about how this all relates to the strictly dominant strategies
    we saw in Chapter 2. In the Prisoner's Dilemma, <m>\text{talk}</m>
    strictly dominates <m>\text{refuse}</m> for both executives, so
    eliminating dominated strategies would have found
    <m>(\text{talk}, \text{talk})</m> too, which is exactly the Nash
    equilibrium!
  </p>

  <p>
    On the other hand, Stag Hunt has no dominant strategies at all, which
    might seem odd since <m>(\text{stag}, \text{stag})</m> gives a payoff of
    <m>(2,2)</m>, the best possible outcome for both players! Check on your
    own, using the definition, that <m>\text{stag}</m> is not a dominant
    strategy. On top of that, Stag Hunt has a second Nash equilibrium, which
    gives a worse payoff of <m>(1,1)</m> to both players.
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-2">
    <title>Section 3.2, Finding Nash Equilibria by Inspection</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.2.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Subsection 3.2.1 can be read very quickly, as we just worked
            through the Prisoner's Dilemma.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Subsection 3.2.2 on Stag Hunt can also be read somewhat quickly,
            since we just worked through the math. The book does give
            additional context and reflection, though, and it defines a
            <term>pure coordination game</term> as a game with several Nash
            equilibria, one of which is best for all players.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Read Subsections 3.2.3 (Chicken) and 3.2.4 (Battle of the Sexes)
            carefully. Both have two Nash equilibria, and as you read, pay
            attention to how the two players feel about them.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <exercise xml:id="check-chicken-bos">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Chicken, Battle of the Sexes, and Stag Hunt all have two Nash
        equilibria. What separates the first two games from Stag Hunt?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The equilibria in Chicken and Battle of the Sexes are not strict,
            while the ones in Stag Hunt are.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            All of these equilibria are strict. In Battle of the Sexes, for
            instance, if Alice leaves the concert while Bob stays there, her
            payoff drops from <m>2</m> to <m>0</m>, a strict decrease.
            Strictness isn't what distinguishes these games.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            In Stag Hunt both players prefer the same one of the two
            equilibria, while in Chicken and Battle of the Sexes each player
            prefers a different one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. In Stag Hunt the players agree about which equilibrium
            they want and the difficulty is getting there together. In Chicken
            and Battle of the Sexes they disagree, so there's a genuine
            conflict over which equilibrium should happen. That's why Stag
            Hunt is called a pure coordination game and the other two are not.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            In Chicken and Battle of the Sexes, a player can reach their
            preferred equilibrium by changing strategy on their own.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            If that were true, the profile they started at would not have been
            a Nash equilibrium in the first place. That's exactly what the
            definition rules out. Getting from one equilibrium to the other
            requires both players to move.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="reflect-alice">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Section 3.2.4 ends by asking you a question directly. If you were
        Alice, which event would you go to, and why? There's no single right
        answer here! Notice that the definition of Nash equilibrium gives you
        no help at all in choosing between the two equilibria, which is a gap
        in the theory rather than a gap in your understanding. Chapter 5 comes
        back to it.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-1" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.1" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>